.TH bad "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
bad \- maintain bad block list
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBbad \fIoption filesystem \fB[\fIblock ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
bad -- maintain bad block list
Usage:	bad option filesystem [ block ... ]
Options:
	a	Add blocks
	d	Delete blocks
	l	List blocks
.HE
A disk may have bad blocks: portions of the medium that are flawed
and cannot reliably be read or written.
.B bad
maintains the list of bad blocks kept for the given
.IR filesystem ,
which must be a block-special file.
The mandatory
.I option
argument is one of the following letters:
.IP \fBa\fR
Add each given
.I block
to the bad-block list.
.IP \fBc\fR
Rewrite the bad-block file without adding or deleting any blocks.
.IP \fBd\fR
Delete each given
.I block
from the bad-block list.
.IP \fBl\fR
List all blocks currently on the bad-block list; no
.I block
arguments are taken.
.PP
Each
.I block
may also be given as a range
.IB b1 \-\fIb2\fR,
meaning every block from
.I b1
through
.I b2
inclusive.
.PP
.B bad
merely adds a block to, or removes a block from, the bad-block list;
it does not free any i-node that may already reference a block being
added.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR icheck ,
.B mkfs
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B bad
prints a message and exits if
.I filesystem
cannot be opened, if a block given to
.B a
is already on the list, or if a block given to
.B d
is not on the list.
